\\text{ times}}=0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139313 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139314 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139313 #edb/ref 1061 174935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139313 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c0f28c5d-f43b-501f-8bff-e540fd9f4ed1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139313 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186139702 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139314 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139315 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139314 #edb/ref 1061 174936 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139314 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a8a467bd-8210-5b7a-b178-dbc650740db0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139314 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186138401 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139315 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139316 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139315 #edb/ref 1061 174940 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139315 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "dcb4b4ef-c7f3-590f-b3b4-e20adba6c4f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139315 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186139743 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139316 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139316 #edb/ref 1061 174941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139316 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a90baf5a-9d3a-5ef4-badb-989c18ead40c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139316 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186138402 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139317 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139317 #edb/ref 1061 174944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139317 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b741f0f4-7d97-5992-8675-c44f666b1b9f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139317 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186138403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139318 #edb/ref 1061 174947 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139318 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a3261c23-f926-5ca7-ab3c-8a935b0af579" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139318 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186138404 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139702 #edb/ref 1064 "To evaluate a power, we write it out with multiplication signs and then compute the resulting product.\n\nSo, to find the value of $9^2,$ we first write it out with multiplication signs as follows:\n![](images/c7/c7e4007e9b80b07aca028ffc9bf8f5c4354bafb4e15dd0ce7e474279e364e1a3.svg)\n\nThen, we compute the product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{9}^{2}=9\\times 9=81\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRaising a number to the power of $2$ is known as **squaring** a number. Therefore, the following are equivalent.\n\n- $9^2 = 81$\n\n- The **square** of $9$ is $81.$\n\n- $9$ raised to the *second* power equals $81.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139702 #edb/ref 1065 8587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139702 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c1b4655c-0ae1-50ce-a37f-b3d77fdc30d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139702 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139743 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's evaluate the following exponent:\n\n$$\n2^{\\color{red}{3}}\n$$\n\nWe start by writing our exponent as repeated multiplication:\n![](images/fe/fea572ac021fd3eabd4d5b1ade6692cbb1507d993b16616f63b8aa78259d3ade.svg)\n\nNow, we compute the product. \n\nSince $2 \\times 2 = 4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{2\\times 2}_{⏟}}_{4}\\times 2 & =4\\times 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${\\color{blue}{4}}\\times 2=8,$ we have $2^3=8.$\n\nRaising a number to the power of $3$ is known as **cubing** a number. Therefore, the following are equivalent.\n\n- $2^3 = 8$\n\n- The **cube** of $2$ is $8.$\n\n- $2$ raised to the *third* power equals $8.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139743 #edb/ref 1065 9457 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139743 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "2cf5f36c-da57-5e4b-91e8-76c86e67837f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139743 #edb/ref 1067 "The Cube of a Number" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186429819 #edb/ref 1080 "9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186429819 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186429819 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "00542389-ab01-5496-b4cb-a8421904c56d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186429820 #edb/ref 1080 "25" #e